        {
          "id": "<REDACTED_UUID>",
          "conversation_id": "<REDACTED>",
          "user_id": "<REDACTED>",
          "role": "assistant",
          "content": "**Case Identified** \nCTA Case No. 9711 – Dizon Farms Produce, Inc. v. CIR\n\n**Facts / Context** \nIn this case, the taxpayer, Dizon Farms Produce, Inc., contested a deficiency tax assessment issued by the Bureau of Internal Revenue (BIR). The taxpayer argued that the assessment was invalid due to the lack of a proper pre-assessment notice (PAN), which is a critical component of the due process in tax assessments.\n\n**Issue** \nThe primary issue was whether the BIR's failure to issue a pre-assessment notice (PAN) rendered the deficiency tax assessment null and void.\n\n**Ruling / Holding** \nThe Court of Tax Appeals (CTA) ruled in favor of the taxpayer, stating that the absence of a PAN violated the taxpayer's due process rights. The court emphasized that tax assessments issued without adhering to the due process requirements are considered null and void.\n\n**Doctrine** \nThe ruling established that the issuance of a pre-assessment notice is a mandatory requirement for valid tax assessments. The taxpayer must be given an opportunity to respond to the findings of the BIR before any formal assessment is made.\n\n**Application** \nIn applying this doctrine, the CTA highlighted that the BIR must follow the procedural requirements set forth in the relevant regulations, specifically noting that the taxpayer has a right to be informed of the basis for the tax assessment and to contest it before any final determination is made.\n\n**Status / Limits** \nThis case underscores the importance of due process in tax assessments and serves as a precedent for similar cases where taxpayers challenge the validity of assessments based on procedural grounds. The ruling is limited to the context of due process in tax assessments and does not address other substantive tax issues.\n\nIn summary, while the specific BIR revenue memorandum circular requested by the taxpayer could not be located, the principles established in CTA Case No. 9711 provide a clear framework regarding the necessity of due process in tax assessments, which is grounded in the provisions of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC).\n\nSource limitation: A governing authority was not directly located. Displayed sources are related, supporting, or secondary only. They are not the controlling basis for the answer.",
